---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import { highlight } from '../lib/highlight'
import { HowStencil } from '../islands/HowStencil'
import { WS_STYLE, SILLON_STYLE, WS_REACT, SILLON_REACT, WS_RENDER, SILLON_GL, SILLON_GROUPS } from '../why-snippets'
import { AFTERGLOW } from '../effects'
import { col, meta, chapterN, lede, ledeTitle, ledeText, pageEnd, section } from '../cls'

// Two approaches, one above the other.
const versus = 'mt-10 grid grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)] gap-5 [&_pre]:text-[13px]'
const versusWho = `${meta} mb-2 block`
---
<Page title="Why sillon" description="How sillon differs from wavesurfer.js: styling, components on the waveform, effects, grouped comments, and when to use wavesurfer.js instead." page="why">
  <section class={`${col} pt-[72px] narrow:pt-12`} aria-labelledby="doc-title">
    <h1 id="doc-title" class="text-[40px]/[1.12] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-balance narrow:text-[32px]">Why sillon</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-text text-[19px]/[1.55] text-ink-2 narrow:text-[17px]">
      sillon and wavesurfer.js both draw audio waveforms in the browser. The differences are in how they style bars,
      place components on a waveform, render custom graphics, and group comments.
    </p>
  </section>

  <main class={`${col} ${pageEnd} [&_code]:text-ink`}>
    <section id="styling" class="mt-[72px] scroll-mt-[76px]">
      <div class={lede}>
        <span class={chapterN}>01</span>
        <h2 class={ledeTitle}>Styling</h2>
        <p class={ledeText}>wavesurfer.js draws bars on a canvas, so colors are JavaScript options. Changing them for a dark theme, for example, requires <code>setOptions</code>. sillon uses the bars as a stencil over <code>Waveform.Track</code> and <code>Waveform.Progress</code>. Their CSS backgrounds show through.</p>
      </div>
      <HowStencil client:only="react"><div slot="fallback" class="h-[140px]" aria-hidden="true"></div></HowStencil>
      <div class={versus}>
        <div><span class={versusWho}>wavesurfer.js</span><div data-code set:html={highlight(WS_STYLE, 'javascript')} /></div>
        <div><span class={versusWho}>sillon</span><div data-code set:html={highlight(SILLON_STYLE, 'tsx')} /></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="react" class={section}>
      <div class={lede}>
        <span class={chapterN}>02</span>
        <h2 class={ledeTitle}>Markers and regions</h2>
        <p class={ledeText}>With <code>@wavesurfer/react</code>, wavesurfer.js plugins create the elements for markers and regions. Their content is a string or DOM element. In sillon, <code>Waveform.Marker</code> and <code>Waveform.Region</code> are React components that render your JSX.</p>
      </div>
      <div class={versus}>
        <div><span class={versusWho}>wavesurfer.js with @wavesurfer/react</span><div data-code set:html={highlight(WS_REACT, 'tsx')} /></div>
        <div><span class={versusWho}>sillon</span><div data-code set:html={highlight(SILLON_REACT, 'tsx')} /></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="effects" class={section}>
      <div class={lede}>
        <span class={chapterN}>03</span>
        <h2 class={ledeTitle}>Effects that follow the music</h2>
        <p class={ledeText}>wavesurfer.js's <code>renderFunction</code> draws bars on a canvas when the waveform loads, zooms, or resizes. sillon's <code>Waveform.GL</code> runs GLSL for every bar on each frame during playback. The shader receives each bar's height, the time since the playhead passed it, and the song's current loudness. The <a href="/#effects">home-page effects</a> use this.</p>
      </div>
      <div class={versus}>
        <div><span class={versusWho}>wavesurfer.js</span><div data-code set:html={highlight(WS_RENDER, 'javascript')} /></div>
        <div><span class={versusWho}>sillon</span><div data-code set:html={highlight(SILLON_GL(AFTERGLOW), 'tsx')} /></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="groups" class={section}>
      <div class={lede}>
        <span class={chapterN}>04</span>
        <h2 class={ledeTitle}>Comments that group as you zoom</h2>
        <p class={ledeText}>On a long track, comments can end up at the same pixels. With wavesurfer.js, you write the code that merges overlapping markers and separates them again on its <code>zoom</code> event. <code>useGroups</code> handles both and re-renders only when the grouping changes. The <a href="/#comments">home-page comments</a> use it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class={versus}>
        <div><span class={versusWho}>sillon</span><div data-code set:html={highlight(SILLON_GROUPS, 'tsx')} /></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="tradeoffs" class={section}>
      <div class={lede}>
        <span class={chapterN}>05</span>
        <h2 class={ledeTitle}>When to use wavesurfer.js</h2>
        <p class={ledeText}>Use wavesurfer.js if you need a spectrogram, volume envelopes or separate left and right channels, which sillon doesn't have; if your app doesn't use React; or if you want a library with years of production use.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Page>
